      "translation": {
        "english_text": "Final words? To whom shall I say them! To the key standing there, thirsting to drink my life? To the knife waiting to throttle my neck? Or to you, who have come to relish the sight of my flowing blood? Whoever I speak to, fine! Whoever hears, fine? At least in my final hour, let my heartbeat pound in fury and then fall still! Let my eyes blaze once and then go dark! Let my blood heat and boil, then turn cold! O rich land of noble Tamil that lived as the ground of justice and the furnace of integrity! Why have you become the dwelling of cheats, the abode of conspiracy? O golden motherland that bore only purity, love and truth! Why do you bear traitors, wicked men and villains? Is the only answer I receive to all these questions the sacrificial altar! Sacrificial altar!! Is that this terrible word? O Kurinji country where cruelty dances its tandava! Is it not enough that you kill me and grow fat by sucking my blood? Must you torment my Rani's life too? You tore my beloved away from me and locked the mother who bore me in the underground prison! You are tearing my heart to shreds! You are filled with remorse! Have you not even a sesame-seed's worth of pity, charity or compassion? She loved me, gave me life, said, `Even if the realm falls, I will not forget you`; the flower-tressed woman, the flower-breeze of the flower-garden, has gone somewhere! Even at the foot of the cremation ground, I have become a sinner unable to meet her! From the age I gained awareness I longed, wondering who the father and mother who bore me were! I did not even properly see the face of my mother, whose eyes shone as she spoke loving words, `My son`! Must I die so soon? Are the wrongdoers to relish that joy? I was born in this soil! On this soil I babbled as a child, crawled and played! On this soil I am about to fall as a corpse. In this land that lets those who come live, must a native of the land die unjustly? Must an innocent man die? Must one who does not even know how to conceive of cruelty die? Let my death be a lesson to you. From now on, see that no innocent person's bones are crushed on this sacrificial altar. Put on the sacrificial altar the arrogance, the conceit, the government law that says, `To labour, anything whatsoever may be done`, the gang of traitors that burns incense to it, Durjayan's gang. Smash the backbone of conspiracy to pieces. Pulverize treachery. Bring injustice down atom by atom..........",
        "mode": "prose-faithful",
        "notes": [
          "The source reads `சாவி` in the opening rhetorical question; the English keeps its literal `key` rather than silently correcting it to a contextually expected death-word.",
          "The source phrase `உழைக்க என்னவேண்டுமானாலும் செய்துவிடலாம்` is compressed/irregular; English stays close with `To labour, anything whatsoever may be done` rather than emending the Tamil.",
          "This immutable utterance spans PDF 19–21 and remains one English unit; physical Tamil word splits are not reproduced in English."
        ]
      }
    },
